<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vienna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vienna is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and was for centuries the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. It withstood Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683, the second of which was broken with the help of a Polish-Lithuanian relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a>. As capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> it became a centre of art, music and modern thought, and it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1814–1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="Vienna" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Superbass · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schoenbrunn_philharmoniker_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
